      "record": "45-51",
      "detail": "5th in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Toronto Blue Jays' summer slump has turned into a cold reality, with <strong>falling to the Padres 5-4 on Sunday to cap a split that left them 45-51 and 12 games behind in the AL East</strong> sealing a two-game losing streak that erases any hint of contender momentum. After stealing one from San Diego with a 5-3 victory on July 10, the offense again showed life before imploding in the ninth, exposing the same inconsistency that has plagued them since May. Kevin Gausman, now 4-8 with a 4.33 ERA, took the loss in the rubber match, while the bullpen - already a question mark - failed to hold a late lead. Dylan Cease, the Jays' All-Star starter, struck out three in a flawless first frame, a bright spot in a season that's otherwise drifting. <strong>Friday's 7:15 PM ET matchup against the White Sox offers a chance to reset, but the real test is whether this team can stop watching the division slip away.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated July 15, 2026"
      },
